course: dictionary
lesson: 9191
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. A sock is a soft piece of clothing you wear on your foot, inside a shoe. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer means.
explain: «If you put on a clean pair of socks every day, you need 7 pairs for a week.» Here socks are things you count, and they come in pairs. So 7 pairs means 14 socks, and you can check by counting: 2, 4, 6 and so on.
explain: «Socks on smooth wooden stairs can slip; shoes or bare feet grip better.» The clues are stairs, slip and grip, so sock means clothing for your foot. Ask yourself: does my meaning fit the sentence?
explain: To sock someone is an informal word that means to hit them. It is used in chatty speech, not in formal writing, so check the other words in the sentence before you decide which meaning is right.
fact: A sock is a soft piece of clothing worn on the foot, inside a shoe.
fact: Socks come in pairs: a pair is 2 socks.
fact: In a maths sum, «5 pairs» of socks is 10 socks.
fact: To sock someone (informal) means to hit them.
fact: Odd socks are two socks that do not match.
exercise: «If you put on a clean pair of socks every day, you need 7 pairs for a week.» How many socks is that? | *14 | 2 | 21 | 7
exercise: «Socks on smooth wooden stairs can slip; shoes or bare feet grip better.» What is a sock in this sentence? | A sweet | *Soft clothing worn on the foot inside a shoe | A kind of stair | A type of wet weather
exercise: What is a pair of socks? | One sock | *Two socks | Three socks | Four socks
test: «Breaking new boots in a little at a time, and changing wet socks at half-time, makes blisters less likely.» What clue shows that socks here are clothing? | Less likely | Half-time, a break in a game | *Changing wet socks, which you take off and put on | Breaking new boots
test: What does to sock someone mean? | *To hit them, informal | To count them in pairs | To wash them in hot water | To put on a pair of socks
exam: «I need 4 pairs of socks for the trip.» How many socks is that? | 2 socks | 1 sock | *8 socks | 4 socks
